REST (HTTP/1.1, JSON):
  POST /orders/42/ship HTTP/1.1
  Host: api.example.com
  Content-Type: application/json

  {"carrier": "ups", "trackingNumber": "1Z999AA10123456784"}

  HTTP/1.1 200 OK
  Content-Type: application/json

  {"orderId": 42, "status": "shipped", "carrier": "ups", "trackingNumber": "1Z999AA10123456784"}

gRPC (.proto contract, compiled into typed stubs):
  service OrderService {
    rpc ShipOrder(ShipOrderRequest) returns (ShipOrderResponse);
  }
  message ShipOrderRequest {
    int64 order_id = 1;
    string carrier = 2;
    string tracking_number = 3;
  }
  message ShipOrderResponse {
    int64 order_id = 1;
    string status = 2;
  }

  // caller's code, after codegen -- looks like a local function call:
  response = order_client.ShipOrder(ShipOrderRequest(order_id=42, carrier="ups", tracking_number="1Z999AA10123456784"))

gRPC server-streaming (native to the protocol):
  service OrderService {
    rpc WatchOrderStatus(OrderRequest) returns (stream OrderStatusUpdate);
  }
  // client opens ONE call, server pushes updates over it as they happen:
  for update in order_client.WatchOrderStatus(OrderRequest(order_id=42)):
      print(update.status)   # "placed", then later "packed", then "shipped", ...

The REST equivalent, simulated with polling (no native push):
  while True:
      response = requests.get("/orders/42/status")
      print(response.json()["status"])
      time.sleep(2)   # client has to guess a polling interval
      # -- wastes requests when nothing changed, and adds up to 2s of
      #    delay noticing a change that already happened

REST: a backend developer silently renames a JSON field
  BEFORE: {"orderId": 42, "total": 19.99}
  AFTER:  {"orderId": 42, "totalAmount": 19.99}   // renamed, no version bump
  -- every existing caller reading response["total"] now gets a KeyError /
     undefined at runtime, discovered whenever that code path next executes

gRPC: the same change attempted in a .proto file
  message OrderResponse {
    int64 order_id = 1;
    double total = 2;      // <- renaming this field number's meaning is a
                            //    breaking change the .proto review process
                            //    is specifically designed to catch and block
  }
  -- caller code was generated FROM this exact schema; a breaking schema
     change requires touching the shared .proto file, which is the natural
     place to require review and a compatibility check before it ships

BAD: pricing discount logic embedded directly in gateway config/scripts
  gateway.on("/api/orders/*", (req, res) => {
    if (req.headers["x-customer-tier"] === "premium") {
      req.body.discountPercent = 15;   // business rule, decided at the edge
    }
    forwardTo("order-service", req);
  });
  -- order-service now has NO say over its own pricing rules
  -- a second backend (e.g. subscription-service) that ALSO needs a
     slightly different premium-tier discount rule has nowhere to put
     its own version of this logic except... also inside the gateway

BETTER: gateway stays thin, forwards a fact, service owns the rule
  gateway.on("/api/orders/*", (req, res) => {
    forwardTo("order-service", req);   // gateway does not decide pricing
  });
  // inside order-service, which OWNS pricing for orders:
  def calculate_order_total(order, customer_tier):
      discount = PRICING_RULES[customer_tier]   # order-service's own rule
      ...
